content.reasoning:
Same error. The target resolves to {}. The error message says dict input.

Hmm, maybe the issue is that the system thinks `<target>` is a separate parameter that is NOT an object. Let me look at the execute function signature:

```
{
  "name": "execute",
  "parameters": {
    "properties": {
      "mode": {...},
      "request": {"type": "object", ...},
      "target": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": false, "anyOf": [{"$ref": "#/components/schemas/EntityRef"}, {"type": "object"}], "description": ...}
    },
    "title": "ExecuteRequest", 
    "type": "object"
  }
}
```

So target is an object, which can be an EntityRef. When I pass `<target>X</target>` or `<target><kind>X</kind><id>Y</id></target>`, it should be an object.

OK let me try a completely DIFFERENT approach for the request content. Use a REST request with proper URL.

Actually wait, let me look at the response of when execute DID work:

`request:req-6417ff/response:resp-5b42b4ea` content:
```
{"status": 404, "body": {"detail": "Not Found"}, "url": "/", "method": "GET"}
```

So the request went through with a method=GET, url=/. Now let me find out how to actually pass method and url.

Hmm. Maybe the issue is that when `<request>...</request>` has content with colons or special chars, the parser splits on them. Let me try with a SIMPLE request body to verify.

