```
"write": {
  "parameters": {
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "properties": {
      "content": {"description": "full new content for the entity. Shape depends on kind.", "type": "object"},
      "entity_ref": {"description": "typed handle, `{kind, id, parent?}`.", "type": "object"},
      "scope": {"default": "local", "description": "ONLY for `kind=\"variable\"`. One of \"local\", \"global\", \"vault\".", "enum": ["local", "global", "vault"], "type": "string"}
    }
  }
}
```

And the system docs again:
"For request (REST): {method, url, headers?, body?, protocol: \"rest\"}"

So `request` content has `method`, `url`, `headers?`, `body?`, `protocol`. All required EXCEPT headers and body.

Hmm, looks like my content with protocol=rest, method=GET, url=... should be valid REST request.

Maybe the system is REJECTING due to additionalProperties: false. Let me check what additionalProperties false does for content. Maybe the schema strips extra fields.

Wait the schema only says `additionalProperties: false` for the WRITE PARAMETER (i.e., write takes content, entity_ref, scope — no extras). For request content, there might be a separate validation.

But my content has ONLY protocol, method, url. Nothing extra.

Hmm. Let me look at something: maybe my URL has SPECIAL CHARS that the parser mishandles. Let me try with NO URL:

